line":null,"rule_met":false,"regression_ok":false,"text":"Tested on the models' own October hindcasts, 1993–2016 (24 years, 5 systems: ECMWF SEAS5, UKMO GloSea6, Météo-France 9, NCEP CFSv2, ECCC CanSIPSv3): the share of a model's first-month error in C that is still there later (pooled, fitted with each year left out) is 0.88 in Oct–Dec, 0.72 in Nov–Jan, 0.55 in Dec–Feb; in E it is 0.90, 0.69, 0.39. The correction applies those weights. Out of sample it changes the multi-model error in E − C in Nov–Jan from 0.62 to 0.59, which is not statistically significant (Wilcoxon over years, p = 0.3). In 1997, the strongest east-based event in the hindcast period, the models' average for Oct–Dec had E +3.9 and C +1.0 against an observed E +4.2 and C +0.3: too central, the same direction as this year's first-month error. A regression calibration of E and C on the hindcasts does not beat the forecast as issued (0.56 in Nov–Jan).","rule":"first-month-corrected leads only if, in the hindcasts, it lowers the multi-model E − C error at the peak season with p < 0.05 (Wilcoxon over years) and raises it in no season"}}